---

<Layout
  title={`How Do Organisms Reproduce? | CBSE Class 10 Science (Biology) | ${provider.name}`}
  description="Exhaustive chapter notes, asexual reproduction mechanisms, flower anatomy & double fertilization, human reproductive physiology, contraception methods, interactive SVG diagrams, and 20 CBSE Board MCQs."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / How Do Organisms Reproduce?
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Science (Biology) · Chapter 7/8</span>
      <h1>How Do Organisms Reproduce? Asexual, Plant &amp; Human Systems</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Master DNA replication, fission, budding, angiosperm double fertilization, the menstrual cycle, placental embryonic exchange, and modern contraception with 20 board exam questions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>1. DNA Replication &amp; Asexual Reproduction</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🧬 DNA Copying &amp; The Importance of Variation</h3>
          <p class="formula">DNA Replication &rarr; Cellular Division &rarr; Variation for Population Survival</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>The Biological Blueprint:</strong> Chromosomes in cell nuclei contain DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid), the master code for manufacturing cellular proteins.</li>
            <li><strong>Inherent Inaccuracy:</strong> No biochemical copying system is 100% reliable. Subtle copying errors result in variations among offspring.</li>
            <li><strong>Niche Survival:</strong> If a bacterial population living in temperate water suddenly faces extreme global warming, most heat-sensitive bacteria perish; only the few pre-existing heat-resistant variants survive, preventing species extinction.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🔬 Asexual Modes: Single-Parent Propagation</h3>
          <p class="formula">Identical Clones · Rapid Multiplicative Proliferation</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Fission:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Binary Fission:</em> In any plane in <em>Amoeba</em>; along a definite longitudinal plane in <em>Leishmania</em> (causative agent of Kala-azar with whip-like flagellum).
              <br/>• <em>Multiple Fission:</em> In <em>Plasmodium</em> (malarial parasite), single cell divides into many daughter cells simultaneously inside a protective cyst.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Fragmentation:</strong> Multicellular filaments like <em>Spirogyra</em> break into smaller fragments upon maturity; each fragment grows into an individual.</li>
            <li><strong>Regeneration:</strong> Fully differentiated organisms like <em>Planaria</em> or <em>Hydra</em> sliced into pieces regenerate complete bodies using specialized proliferating stem cells.</li>
            <li><strong>Budding:</strong> In <em>Hydra</em> and yeast, a small bud develops from repeated cell divisions at one site, detaching when fully mature.</li>
            <li><strong>Spore Formation:</strong> In <em>Rhizopus</em> (bread mould), hyphae produce knob-like sporangia filled with thick-walled spores that resist harsh conditions until moisture is found.</li>
            <li><strong>Vegetative Propagation:</strong> Natural via <em>Bryophyllum</em> leaf notches, potato eyes, ginger rhizomes; artificial via cutting, layering, and grafting.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Plant & Human Sexual Reproduction -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>2. Plant Fertilization &amp; Human Reproductive Anatomy</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌺 Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants</h3>
          <p class="formula">Pollination &rarr; Pollen Tube Growth &rarr; Double Fertilization</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Flower Morphology:</strong>
              <br/>• Male Stamen: Anther (produces pollen grains) + Filament.
              <br/>• Female Carpel/Pistil: Stigma (sticky receptive surface) + Style + Ovary (contains ovules with female egg cell).
              <br/>• Unisexual (Papaya, Watermelon) vs. Bisexual (Mustard, Hibiscus).
            </li>
            <li><strong>Double Fertilization:</strong>
              <br/>1. <em>Syngamy:</em> 1st male gamete (n) fuses with female egg cell (n) &rarr; Diploid Zygote (2n), developing into embryo.
              <br/>2. <em>Triple Fusion:</em> 2nd male gamete (n) fuses with diploid secondary nucleus (2n) &rarr; Triploid Primary Endosperm Nucleus (3n), providing nutrition.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Post-Fertilization Transformations:</strong> Ovule develops a tough coat and becomes the <strong>Seed</strong>; the ovary ripens and enlarges into the <strong>Fruit</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🧑‍🤝‍🧑 Human Reproductive Physiology</h3>
          <p class="formula">Gametogenesis &rarr; Fertilization in Oviduct &rarr; Uterine Implantation</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Male System:</strong> Testes located outside abdominal cavity in the scrotum to maintain 2–2.5°C lower temperature essential for spermatogenesis. Vas deferens carries sperm; prostate and seminal vesicles add alkaline fluids for mobility and nutrition.</li>
            <li><strong>Female System:</strong> Ovaries release one mature ovum every 28 days. Fertilization occurs in the <strong>Fallopian Tube (Oviduct)</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>The Placenta:</strong> A specialized vascular disc embedded in the uterine wall. Contains chorionic villi on embryo's side bathed in maternal blood pools; transports glucose, O₂, and antibodies to the embryo while removing urea and CO₂.</li>
            <li><strong>Menstruation:</strong> When an ovum is unfertilized, the thickened spongy uterine lining breaks down, shedding blood and mucus for 3–5 days.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Reproductive Health Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>3. Reproductive Health &amp; Contraceptive Strategy</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🛡️ Contraceptive Methods &amp; Mechanisms</h3>
          <p class="formula">Prevention of Unwanted Pregnancies &amp; Population Stabilization</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Mechanical Barrier Methods:</strong> Condoms (male/female) or cervical diaphragms physically block sperm from entering the female genital tract. <em>Condoms additionally protect against STDs</em>.</li>
            <li><strong>Hormonal/Chemical Methods:</strong> Oral pills change the body's hormonal balance, preventing egg release (ovulation). Can cause minor hormonal side effects.</li>
            <li><strong>Intrauterine Contraceptive Devices (IUCDs):</strong> Loop or Copper-T inserted into the uterus by a doctor to prevent implantation.</li>
            <li><strong>Surgical Methods (Permanent Sterilization):</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Vasectomy:</em> Small portion of vas deferens is cut and tied in males; blocks sperm release.
              <br/>• <em>Tubectomy:</em> Small portion of fallopian tubes is cut and tied in females; blocks egg transit.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚠️ Sexually Transmitted Diseases (STDs)</h3>
          <p class="formula">Pathogenic Transmission via Unprotected Intercourse</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Bacterial STDs:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Gonorrhoea</em> (caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>).
              <br/>• <em>Syphilis</em> (caused by <em>Treponema pallidum</em>). Curable with antibiotic regimens if detected early.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Viral STDs:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Genital Warts</em> (Human Papillomavirus / HPV).
              <br/>• <em>HIV-AIDS</em> (Human Immunodeficiency Virus): Destroys helper T-lymphocytes, compromising the immune system; incurable but manageable with antiretrovirals.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Female Foeticide:</strong> Misuse of prenatal diagnostic techniques (ultrasound) to abort female fetuses; prohibited strictly under the PCPNDT Act to maintain balanced sex ratios.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: Floral Fertilization &amp; Uterine Gestation</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <article class="card visual-card">
